\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Architecture of the sparse-attention L-TAE-S. Before the multi-head temporal attention, a TabNet-style sparse feature gate (highlighted) applies a per-head \texttt  {sparsemax} mask that selects a small subset of embedding channels, with a relaxation prior ($\gamma =1.5$) discouraging heads from re-selecting the same channels. This block is the only architectural difference from the L-TAE; five seeds are ensembled and aggregated to field level.}}{10}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ltae_s}{{2}{10}{Architecture of the sparse-attention L-TAE-S. Before the multi-head temporal attention, a TabNet-style sparse feature gate (highlighted) applies a per-head \texttt {sparsemax} mask that selects a small subset of embedding channels, with a relaxation prior ($\gamma =1.5$) discouraging heads from re-selecting the same channels. This block is the only architectural difference from the L-TAE; five seeds are ensembled and aggregated to field level}{figure.2}{}}
